🧠 1. What are Transformations?

	Transformations are mathematical operations applied to variables (features) to change their distribution — often to make data more normal-like, reduce skewness, or stabilize variance.

	They’re very common in feature engineering, statistical modeling, and machine learning preprocessing.

⚙️ 2. Function Transformation (General Concept)
	Definition:

	A function transformation means applying any mathematical function to your data to change its scale, spread, or shape.

	💡 Purpose:

	To reduce skewness

	To stabilize variance

	To make relationships more linear

	To improve model performance


⚡ 3. Power Transformation (Special Type of Function Transformation)

	A power transformation is a specific kind of function transformation where you apply a power function to make it more Gaussian (normal).

	These are used to stabilize variance and reduce skewness.

🧩 4. Two Common Power Transformations
	(A) Box-Cox Transformation

	Only works with positive values (> 0)

	Searches for the best λ (lambda) value to make data as normal as possible


	(B) Yeo-Johnson Transformation

	Works with both positive and negative values

	A generalization of Box-Cox


Many models (like Linear Regression, Logistic Regression) assume normally distributed residuals.

Tree-based models (like Random Forests) don’t need it, but scaling helps convergence for others (like SVM, KNN, Neural Nets).

Helps handle heteroscedasticity (unequal variance across features).


🧠 Bonus: Model Selection Strategy (Step-by-Step)

	Start simple → Linear / Logistic Regression

	Check residuals → If not linear → move to tree-based

	If data is large and complex → try XGBoost or LightGBM

	If data is unstructured (image/text) → use Deep Learning

	Compare models using cross-validation → choose best performance